#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string ticker;
    double price;
    std::cin >> ticker >> price;
    std::cout << "Ticker: " << ticker << '\n';
    std::cout << "Price: " << price << '\n';
    return 0;
}

screens.prose:
This time `main.cpp` has no `return 0;`, and you build with `-Wall`, which asks for extra warnings.

```cpp
int main() {
    int qty = 100;
    std::cout << "Hello, market" << '\n';
}
```

Most Python habits stop the build: a missing `;`, `print(...)`, indentation instead of braces. One does not.

```cpp
int signals = 3;
if (signals = 0) {
    std::cout << "flat\n";
}
std::cout << signals << '\n';
```
